Perfect timing — your spec docs already track the open items (docs/zoms-api-reference.md §5 and docs/architecture.md §12), and building the facade surfaced a few more. Here's the consolidated, prioritized list you can send them, grouped so the meeting can move top-down.

Tier 1 — Blockers (nothing connects until these are answered)
Authentication & endpoints

Token endpoint URLs for ZOMS, CAT and PROD — are they the same auth server as Paze (https://auth.wallet.{cat.}earlywarning.io/token), or ZOMS-specific? (Our current values are copied from Paze docs — a guess.)
aud (audience) claim value our client_assertion JWT must carry.
Scope — is maintenance-event the correct scope string?
mTLS — is mutual TLS required on the token endpoint and/or the API endpoints (answer separately)? If so, which CA chain do we trust, and do we present a client certificate — how is that keypair provisioned to us?
Onboarding confirmation — confirm our client_id, and the kid of the public key you have on file for us (we understand our public key is already registered as a JWKS — please confirm it's active for ZOMS).
Confirm the CAT + PROD API base URLs (https://api.zelle.cat.earlywarning.io/zoms, https://api.zelle.earlywarning.com/zoms).
JWT clock-skew tolerance on iat/nbf/exp, and token TTL / refresh behavior (we see expires_in: 1800 — is it just re-request, no refresh token?).
Tier 2 — Payment-safety critical (controls live MQ holds)
Idempotency & retry semantics
8. Does idempotency-id apply to start/complete/cancel, or only schedule?
9. Are start/complete/cancel safe to retry (idempotent) if we send a request but never receive your response? (This decides whether we can auto-retry or must stop and reconcile — the single biggest design question for us.)
10. Must request-id be unique per attempt (new value on retry) while idempotency-id stays constant?
11. Schedule replay with the same idempotency-id but a different body — rejected, or ignored?
12. Replay after a client crash/timeout — do you echo the original 201 (and the same maintenanceEventId), or return a duplicate error?

Operational safety
13. Manual escalation path to release held MQ messages if /complete is unreachable during an active window — what is it? (Critical: if the window is IN_PROGRESS with EWS_HOLD and we can't reach you, how do we get payments flowing again?)

Tier 3 — Contract completeness
Schedule 201 response body — full schema; is maintenanceEventId returned synchronously, and under what key? (Determines whether our "pending upstream id" fallback ever triggers.)
Error response body shape + error-code catalog — only success codes were in the docs. Specifically, how do you respond to: double-start, complete-without-start, cancel-after-start, idempotency-replay — and which are retryable vs. terminal vs. already-converged?
Read/status endpoint — is there a GET /v1/events/{id} or a list/status API to query event state, or any state-change webhook? (Right now our only knowledge of your state is the response to the four POSTs.)
Rate limits / concurrency limits and the Retry-After behavior on 429.
Scheduling constraints — minimum lead time, maximum window length, and overlap policy (can two windows overlap?).
Tier 4 — Field & behavior clarifications
ewsHold — the exact operational difference between EWS_HOLD and SELF_HOLD; specifically, under SELF_HOLD, what does EWS do with messages during the window?
suppressDuplicatePayments — exact effect on "On New Payment" notifications.
networkNotificationId — what is a "Network notification record," and when should we link one?
How I'd run the meeting: get Tier 1 fully answered (you literally cannot test in CAT without it), then push hardest on #9 and #13 in Tier 2 — those two shape how safely the whole facade behaves around real money. Everything in Tier 3–4 you can also chase over email if the meeting runs short.

